154 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (The findings indicate a high level of student engagement with AI tools. ChatGPT was the most widely used tool, reported by 36 respondents \(90%\), followed by Google Gemini with 30 respondents \(75%\). Seventeen respondents \(42.5%\) reported using AI several times a day. In relation to academic performance, 27 respondents \(67.5%\) reported either some improvement or significant improvement since using AI, while 11 respondents \(27.5%\) reported either some or no significant change in the negative direction. The major concerns identified by respondents were reduced critical thinking, lack of originality or creativity, and incorrect information. Despite these concerns, 35 respondents \(87.5%\) supported allowing AI in colleges in some form, mainly through clear guidelines or restrictions on particular academic activities.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
155 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (Overall, the survey presents AI as a widely used academic resource that students generally perceive as useful, while simultaneously identifying concerns related to dependence, critical thinking, originality, accuracy, and responsible use. The findings suggest that the central issue emerging from the survey is not simply whether students use AI, but how they use it within the learning process.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
