338 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (This study analysed a 40-response student survey concerning the use of Artificial Intelligence in education. The findings indicate that AI is already an important academic resource for many of the surveyed students.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
339 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (ChatGPT was reported by 90% of respondents, while Google Gemini was reported by 75%. The largest group of respondents used AI several times a day. Students used AI across a broad range of academic activities, particularly for understanding difficult concepts, solving problems, preparing for examinations, summarising materials, conducting research, writing, coding, and generating ideas. )
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
340 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (The survey also indicates a generally positive perception of AI's academic usefulness. A total of 67.5% of respondents reported some or significant improvement in academic performance since using AI. However, these results represent perceptions rather than independently measured academic outcomes.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
341 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (At the same time, students identified substantial concerns. Reduced critical thinking was the most frequently selected concern, followed by lack of originality or creativity and incorrect information. These concerns were reinforced by open-ended responses describing the risks of excessive dependence and the possibility that students may rely on AI instead of engaging in independent thinking. )
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
342 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (The policy findings are particularly notable. Although students expressed concerns about AI, 87.5% selected a response supporting some form of college permission for AI use. The largest group preferred AI to be allowed with clear guidelines.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
343 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 149 0 R
/E (Therefore, the central finding of this survey is that students do not appear to view AI simply as either beneficial or harmful. Their responses portray AI as a powerful academic tool whose value depends substantially on how it is incorporated into the learning process. The survey points toward a model in which AI can assist students while independent thinking, verification, originality, and personal academic responsibility remain important components of student work.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
